\section{Block-Based Programming Environments}
Block-based programming environments have emerged as a dominant approach for introducing
computational concepts to novice programmers, particularly children. By representing code as
interlocking visual blocks, these environments eliminate syntax errors and reduce the cognitive
load associated with textual programming languages \cite{weintrop2015, maloney2010}.

Empirical studies of Scratch-based learning further suggest that block-based programming can
support the development of programming concepts, problem solving, and curricular integration
when tasks are appropriately scaffolded~\cite{meerbaum2013, kalelioglu2014, saezLopez2016}.

\subsection{Scratch}
Scratch, developed at the MIT Media Lab, is perhaps the most widely recognized block-based programming
environment for children \cite{resnick2009}. Launched in 2007, Scratch emphasizes creative
expression through the creation of interactive stories, games and animations.
The platform has been very successful, with millions of projects shared in its online community.

However, Scratch's open-ended nature presents challenges for structured classroom instruction:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Projects can become arbitrarily complex, making assessment difficult
	\item No built-in auto-grading or immediate feedback mechanism
	\item The large block library can overwhelm beginners
	\item Cloud-based storage raises privacy concerns for European users
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Microsoft MakeCode}
Microsoft MakeCode extends block-based programming to physical computing, supporting hardware platforms such as
the \textit{micro:bit}, Arduino, and Adafruit Circuit Playground~\cite{ball2019, devine2019, austin2020}. MakeCode provides a polished development
experience with integrated simulation, block-to-text transitions, and hardware deployment.

While MakeCode excels at hardware integration, its complexity and hardware focus limit its applicability
for pure algorithmic thinking exercises. Classroom studies around the micro:bit ecosystem emphasize creative and physical-computing use cases rather than tightly constrained worksheet-style tasks~\cite{sentance2017cool, sentance2017teaching, bachinger2022}. The platform is designed for project-based learning rather
than structured, gradeable exercises.

\subsection{Google Blockly}
Google Blockly is not a complete programming environment but rather a library for building
block-based programming interfaces \cite{blockly}. MakeCode for example uses it under the hood.
Blockly provides:
\begin{itemize}
	\item A robust block rendering and connection engine
	\item Extensible code generators for multiple target languages
	\item Internationalization support with block label translations
	\item Accessibility features including keyboard navigation
	\item Customizable toolboxes and block categories
\end{itemize}

Blockly's library nature makes it ideal for building custom educational platforms where the exercise structure, grading
logic and user interface can be tailored to specific needs.
BlockQuiz leverages Blockly as its foundation, building a complete learning platform around the library.

\subsection{Code.org}
Code.org offers curriculum-aligned computer science courses with structured exercises and automatic grading.
The platform has been widely adopted in the United States and provides a good scaffold for learners.
However, Code.org's closed ecosystem nature and US-centric curriculum limits its applicability for Austrian schools,
and its cloud-only deployment model raises data protection concerns.

