\section{Automated Assessment in Programming Education}
\label{sec:auto-grading}

Automated assessment of programming exercises has been an active research area since
the 1960s, with early classroom systems already described by Hollingsworth, Naur, and Forsythe and Wirth~\cite{hollingsworth1960, naur1964, forsythe1965}; later surveys document the continued development of this research area~\cite{alaMutka2005, douce2005, ihantola2010, messer-neil}. Modern auto-graders employ various strategies to evaluate student code,
each with distinct advantages and limitations.

\subsection{Test-Based Grading}
The most common approach executes student code against a set of test cases~\cite{douce2005, alaMutka2005},
comparing actual output to expected outpus. This approach is well stuited for Input/Output (I/O) exercises, where correctness
can be defined in terms of input-output pairs.

Key considerations for test-based grading include:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Visible vs. hidden tests}: Visible tests provide immediate feedback and learning scaffolds, while hidden tests
	      prevent hardcoding of solutions.
	\item \textbf{Output normalization}: Handling whitespace, case sensitivity, and formatting differences.
	\item \textbf{Partial credit}: Awarding points for partially correct solutions.
	\item \textbf{Feedback quality}: Providing meaningful error messages rather than simple pass/fail indicators.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{State-Based Grading}
For visual programming exercises such as turtle graphics, state-based grading evaluates the final state of the execution
environment rather than textual output. This includes:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Position and orientation of graphical elements
	\item Command sequences executed during the program
	\item Canvas or grid state at program termination
\end{itemize}

State-based grading allows for more flexible solutions, as multiple command sequences can produce equivalent final states~\cite{malmi2004}.
BlockQuiz implements both exact command matching and final state comparison with configurable tolerance thresholds.


\subsection{Benefits of Immediate Feedback}
Research on formative assessment consistently demonstrates the value of immediate feedback for learning
\cite{blackWiliam1998, hattie2007, shute2008}. Auto-grading enables instant feedback that would be impossible
to provide manually in a classroom setting, allowing students to:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Iterate quickly on solutions
	\item Identify and correct misunderstandings immediately
	\item Progress at their own pace without waiting for teacher review
\end{itemize}

\section{Learning Theory Foundations}
The design of BlockQuiz is informed by learning theories that guide decisions in exercise design, feedback mechanisms and scaffolding approaches.

\subsection{Constructionism}
Seymour Papert's constructionism builds on Piaget's constructivism, arguing that learning is particualarly effective when learners
Are actively constructing artifacts that are personally meaningfull \cite{papert1980, constructionism}. Papert's work with Logo and turtle graphics demonstrated how
programming could serve as a medium for exploring mathematical concepts thourhg construction.

Blockquiy embraces constructionist principles by:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Providing immediate visual feedback through turtle graphics (as in \cite{papert1980})
	\item Allowing learners to experiment and observe consequences
	\item Making abstract concepts (angles, distances, repetition) tangible
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Cognitive Load Theory}
Cognitive Load Theory \cite{sweller1988} distinguishes between intrinsic load (inherent to the material),
extraneous load (caused by poor instructional design) and germane load (contributing to learning).
Block-based programming reduces extraneous cognitive load by:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Eliminating syntax errors and the need to memorize keywords
	\item Providing visual scaffoling throuhg block shapes that indicate valid connections
	\item Constraining the problem space through per-exercise toolboxes
\end{itemize}

BlockQuiz's constrained exercise model direcrly applies cognitive load principles by limiting available blocks
to those relevant for each learning objective, reducing the extraneous load of navigating a large block library.

\subsubsection{Zone of Proximal Development}
Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) \cite{Vygotsky1980, Shabani} describes the gap between what a learner can
do independently and what they can achieve with guidance. Eﬀective instruction targets this
zone, providing appropriate scaﬀolding that can be gradually removed.

BlockQuiy implements scaffoling through:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Progressive hin systems that reveal guidance on demand
	\item Starter blcoks that provide partial solutions for complex exercises
	\item Difficulty progression within courses that builds on prior knowledge
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Age-Appropriate Design}
Desigin for children aged 8-12 requrieds understanding developmental psychology and applying pricniples from child-computer interaction research.

\subsubsection{Developmental Considerations}
In classical Piagetian terms, many children in roughly the 7--11/12 age range are in the concrete
operational stage~\cite{PiagetInhelder1969}. This stage is commonly characterized by:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Ability to perform logical operations on concrete objects
	\item Development of conservation and reversibility concepts
	\item More limited abstract reasoning than in later formal-operational thinking
\end{itemize}

This has implications for exercise design:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Younger children (8-9) often benefit from concrect, visual exercises like turtle graphics
	\item Abstract concepts (variables, conditionals) should be introduced with concrete examples
	\item Older children (11-12) may increasingly handle more abstract I/O-style exercises when they are appropriately scaffolded
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Interface Design for Children}
Research on child-computer interaction suggests several design principles \cite{read2008}:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Large targets:} Interactive elements should be large enough for less precise motor control
	\item \textbf{Minimal text:} Instructions should be concise and use simple vocabulary
	\item \textbf{Visual feedback:} Immediate, visual responses to actions
	\item \textbf{Forgiving interfaces:} Undo functionality and tolerance for errors
	\item \textbf{Playful aesthetics:} Engaging visual design appropriate for the age group
\end{itemize}

BlockQuiz applies these principles thorugh large block targets, concise exercise descriptions, immediate visual feedback
from the canvas, and encouraging feedback messages.

\subsection{Privacy and Data Protection}
The General Data Protection Regulation~(\cite{gdpr2016}) imposes strict requirements on processing children's data within the European Union.
These requirements are particularly relevant for educational software deployed in schools. 

\subsubsection{GDPR Requirements for Children's Data}
Key GDPR provisions affecting BlockQuiz include: 
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Article 8:} Parental consent required for children under 1
\end{itemize}
